{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1692361792/14/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"cb2d8a2c\" lines=\"13-26\">\n<description>\nExtend a green path from one grid edge to the opposite, routing around red bars at a distance dictated by their blue pixel count.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = azure (8). Red/blue bars (color 2/1) act as obstacles.\n- A single green (3) pixel sits on a grid border — the path start.\n\nRule:\n- Each bar's keep-out radius equals its number of blue pixels; the path must stay farther than that from every bar cell.\n- The green pixel extends inward toward the opposite edge, turning at right angles to navigate around forbidden zones.\n- After the path is drawn, all blue pixels become red.\n\nKey insight: Blue pixels on a bar encode its danger radius — more blue means a wider no-go zone the path must respect.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"67e490f4\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nFill holes inside a large template frame using shape-matched outside sprites.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. One large monochrome frame object (template).\n- Inside the frame: holes of various shapes (non-frame-color regions).\n- Outside the frame: small monochrome sprites scattered on bg.\n\nRule:\n- Crop to the frame's bounding box; fill entirely with frame color.\n- For each hole, find an outside sprite whose shape matches (rotation/flip allowed).\n- Fill the hole with the matched sprite's color. If multiple sprites match, use the most frequent color.\n\nKey insight: Sprites act as shape-coded color keys — their shape identifies which hole they fill, their color determines the fill color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f18ec8cc\" lines=\"71-86\">\n<description>\nReorder the vertical color-major blocks while preserving every block's internal pixels.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid is divided into vertical blocks, each with a unique dominant color.\nSmall marker pixels from other block colors may appear inside the blocks.\n\nRule\nIf adjacent shared-color marker rows read exactly as the current block order\nfrom right to left, reverse the blocks. Otherwise move the leftmost block to\nthe far right and shift the remaining blocks left.\n\nKey insight\nThe transformation acts on whole vertical blocks, so marker pixels move with\ntheir source block instead of being recolored or independently rearranged.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"fc4aaf52\" lines=\"17-34\">\n<description>\nSplit the two-color object horizontally, swap its colors, and slide the upper half to the right.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one background color and exactly two foreground colors.\n- All foreground pixels form one 4-connected object with an even-height bbox.\n- The object has foreground pixels on both rows adjacent to its horizontal midpoint.\n\nRule:\n- Split the object into upper and lower halves at the midpoint of its bbox.\n- Swap the two foreground colors in both halves.\n- Keep the lower half in place and shift the upper half right until its lower-left\n  boundary pixel is diagonally adjacent to the lower half's upper-right boundary pixel.\n\nKey insight:\n- The object is not copied or reflected; it is broken at the horizontal waist,\n  recolored by exchanging the two visible object colors, then rejoined diagonally.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"3e6067c3\" lines=\"7-20\">\n<description>\nConnect framed squares via colored bridges following a control-pixel sequence.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Border color forms equal-sized framed squares with unique inner fill colors.\n- Control row below squares: colored pixels read left-to-right encode visitation order.\n\nRule:\n- Start at the square matching first control color c0.\n- For each consecutive pair (ci, ci+1), find the ci+1-colored square aligned in the same row or column band, preferring nearest.\n- Draw a bridge colored ci between the pair, spanning the gap with inner-square thickness.\n\nKey insight: Row/column-band alignment plus proximity resolves ambiguity when inner colors repeat.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"d753a70b\" lines=\"81-93\">\n<description>\nRed diamond outlines contract by one layer and gray diamond outlines expand by one layer while other objects stay fixed.\n\nInput structure\n- A 16x16 orange-background grid contains one-cell-thick Manhattan-diamond outlines.\n- Red and gray outlines are the changing objects; other colored diamond outlines are static.\n- Some outlines may be clipped by the grid boundary, but their visible pixels still fit a clipped Manhattan-distance contour.\nRule\n- Clear all red and gray pixels.\n- Red objects are redrawn with the same diamond center and radius minus one.\n- Gray objects are redrawn with radius plus one; an ambiguous edge-clipped growing arc continues away from the nearest perpendicular edge.\nKey insight\n- The task is not about moving whole objects: each changing object is a contour of a diamond, and the visible operation is one step of contour contraction or expansion.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9f41bd9c\" lines=\"10-30\">\n<description>\nMove the side-attached foreground object to the opposite wall, shear its lower rows inward, and paint the crossed floor cells.\n\nInput structure:\n- The top row is the upper background color and the bottom row is the\n  floor color.\n- A single non-background, non-floor object touches exactly one vertical\n  grid edge and rests immediately above the first solid floor row.\n\nRule:\n- Clear the source object to the upper background color.\n- Repaint each object row on the opposite side: the first three relative\n  rows keep the edge alignment, and each lower row shifts one more pixel\n  inward from that side.\n- On the first floor row, repaint the traversed side-to-object segment\n  maroon, stopping just before the final outer bottom support pixel.\n\nKey insight:\n- The side of the source object gives the horizontal direction, while the\n  row index inside the object gives the one-cell-per-row diagonal shear.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"df978a02\" lines=\"57-72\">\n<description>\nLet the largest center-pointing object grow outward while every other object loses its inward tip.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a single dominant background color and four 8-connected monochromatic foreground objects.\n- The objects surround a central background gap and point toward it from cardinal directions.\n- Exactly one object has the largest area.\n\nRule\n- Find the central background gap by minimizing aggregate distance to the four objects.\n- Recolor the nearest-to-center pixel of each non-largest object to background.\n- Add a three-pixel cap in the largest object's color on the side away from the center, centered on the shared row or column.\n\nKey insight\n- The edit is a transfer of prominence around a common center: losers retract at their tips, and the unique largest object extends outward from the center.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"a6953f00\" lines=\"45-63\">\n<description>\nExtract the top-left or top-right 2x2 corner crop selected by the top edge clue.\n\nInput structure\n- The input is a square color grid whose answer is one of the two 2x2 crops\n  touching the top edge and a grid corner.\n- A repeated color on the top row, when present, acts as the primary side cue.\n- Otherwise, the two top corner colors have different whole-grid frequencies.\n\nRule\n- If a top-row color repeats, find the repeated top-row occurrence nearest to\n  a left or right edge and take the 2x2 top-corner crop on that side.\n- If the top row has no repeated color, compare the whole-grid frequencies of\n  the two top-corner colors and take the side whose corner color is rarer.\n\nKey insight\n- The task is not asking for an arbitrary 2x2 search: the decisive information\n  is on the top edge, and it selects which top corner crop to keep.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"54dc2872\" lines=\"52-69\">\n<description>\nMove each marked L-shaped sprite onto the separate pixel matching its marker color.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Foreground has 4-connected multicolor sprites and isolated one-pixel controls.\n- Each sprite has one repeated body color in an L shape plus one embedded marker\n  pixel of a different color.\n\nRule\n- If the embedded marker color also appears as a separate isolated control pixel,\n  translate the whole sprite so the body L-bend lands on that control pixel.\n- Clear each moved sprite from its old position, clear its consumed control pixel,\n  then paste all translated sprites; sprites without a matching control stay put.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker color pairs a sprite with its destination control, while the L-bend\n  gives the orientation-independent anchor cell to place on that destination.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"fd02da9e\" lines=\"26-41\">\n<description>\nReplace each colored corner marker with its corner-specific inward stencil in the same color.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid is 8x8 with a single dominant background color.\n- Foreground pixels are marker pixels, each located at one of the four corners.\n- A marker's color is carried to the stencil selected by its corner position.\n\nRule\n- Erase each corner marker back to the background.\n- Top corner markers become an inward 2x2 block one cell away from the top and side edges.\n- Bottom corner markers become the mirrored three-pixel inward bent stencil shown by the bottom-corner examples.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker position selects the fixed stencil; the marker color only supplies the paint color, so multiple corners apply independently.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"84ba50d3\" lines=\"36-54\">\n<description>\nDrop the blue sprites downward relative to the red divider, with vertical sprites passing through to the bottom.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- One non-background color forms a single full-width horizontal divider row.\n- The other non-background color forms 4-connected sprites above the divider.\n\nRule:\n- Clear the original sprite pixels.\n- A one-column sprite falls to the bottom of its column and deletes the divider\n  pixel it crossed.\n- Any wider sprite falls until its unique widest horizontal row is immediately\n  above the divider row; if the moved sprite overlaps the divider, it covers it.\n\nKey insight:\nThe red row is not a generic floor: only one-column sprites pass through to the\nbottom, while wider sprites are registered by their widest horizontal segment.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ad173014\" lines=\"97-111\">\n<description>\nRecolor each framed interior mask with the color from the next red frame clockwise around the blue connector cycle.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid contains hollow red square frames connected pairwise by blue line components.\n- The blue components connect the frames into one cycle, with every frame touching exactly two blue components.\n- Each frame interior contains background plus exactly one non-background color mask.\n\nRule\n- Sort the red frames clockwise by their centers around the cycle.\n- Keep each frame's interior mask shape in place, but recolor it with the interior color from the next clockwise frame.\n- Leave the red frames, blue connectors, background, and interior background holes unchanged.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
